{#-
  Syrup — router deposit / requestRedeem / cancel-by-id / redeem on Maple's
  public Syrup pools (syrupUSDC, syrupUSDT).

  This is NOT templates/maple/maple.tmpl with different params. Syrup's entry
  path runs through a SyrupRouter — a different target, a different signature,
  and an approve whose spender is the ROUTER rather than the pool — and
  cancellation runs through the pool's withdrawal manager by request id. Three
  of the calls scoped here have no counterpart in maple.tmpl, and maple.tmpl's
  pool.deposit rule is not needed at all.

  Each entry in deposit_assets is the UNDERLYING asset of a Syrup pool. The
  template resolves, per entry and with NO existence guard:
    aliases.maple[syrup_<asset_lower>_pool]
    aliases.maple[syrup_<asset_lower>_router]
    aliases.maple[syrup_<asset_lower>_withdrawal_manager]
  If any alias is missing on the active chain, rendering throws — there is no
  silent fall-through. To grant the Safe access to a Syrup pool, add the asset
  to deposit_assets AND make sure all three aliases exist in
  aliases/<chain>/maple.yaml. Note that aliases.maple.usdc_pool is a DIFFERENT
  pool (MPLhysUSDC1, permissioned) and is never read here.

  Required template params:
    deposit_assets — list of token alias keys, each the underlying asset of a
                     Syrup pool on the active chain. Example: ["USDC"].
                     - aliases.tokens[<key>]                               must exist
                     - aliases.maple[syrup_<key_lower>_pool]               must exist
                     - aliases.maple[syrup_<key_lower>_router]             must exist
                     - aliases.maple[syrup_<key_lower>_withdrawal_manager] must exist
                     Any lookup missing ⇒ render-time error. List each asset
                     once: a repeated entry renders duplicate
                     (address, signature) pairs, which the emit-phase merger
                     rejects.
    deposit_data   — Maple's bytes32 attribution tag, conventionally
                     `0:<integrator-name>`. bytes32("0:railnet") is
                     0x303a7261696c6e65740000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000.
                     Pinned with equal_to on the router deposit, so the Safe's
                     deposits can only ever carry the agreed tag.

  Entry flow (router, single deposit call):
    1. asset.approve(router, assets)         — exact grant
    2. router.deposit(assets, deposit_data)  — the router pulls the underlying
                                               and mints pool shares to the Safe
    3. asset.approve(router, 0)              — zero the leftover allowance
    Steps 1 and 3 are the SAME rule: `amount` is `pass`, so the exact grant and
    the zeroing are both covered by one approve.

  Exit flow (request-then-settle, with a cancellation and a claim path):
    1. pool.requestRedeem(shares, owner=safe)  — escrows the shares into the
                                                 withdrawal manager and queues
                                                 the redemption
    2. …wait for the pool delegate to service the queue…
       Assets land on the Safe automatically — UNLESS the delegate has flagged
       the Safe for manual withdrawal, in which case the serviced shares stay
       on the withdrawal manager and must be claimed with:
       pool.redeem(shares, receiver=safe, owner=safe)
    Instead of waiting, an exact queued request can be cancelled with:
       withdrawalManager.removeSharesById(requestId, sharesToRemove)

  Why cancellation targets the withdrawal manager and not the pool:
    MapleWithdrawalManager.removeSharesById is unprivileged and
    owner-authenticated (`request.owner == msg.sender`), exact by id, and does
    not pass through MaplePool.checkCall — so it is not gated on the pool's
    `P:removeShares` PoolPermissionManager bitmap, which on syrupUSDC is the
    same bitmap as `P:deposit` and would otherwise make cancellation fail
    exactly when Maple has revoked the Safe's deposit rights. pool.removeShares
    is therefore deliberately NOT scoped here.

  PINNED, ROTATION-SENSITIVE TARGET:
    The withdrawal manager is not a fixed property of the pool. It is resolved
    dynamically on-chain via poolManager.withdrawalManager(), and the pool
    delegate can rotate it at any time (spawnWithdrawalManager). This template
    pins whatever the alias holds, so after a rotation the policy still permits
    only the OLD manager and cancellation stops working. On rotation: re-read
    poolManager.withdrawalManager(), update
    aliases/<chain>/maple.yaml → syrup_<asset>_withdrawal_manager, and re-apply
    the policy.

  Output shape (per asset in deposit_assets, four roles — no more):
    - One approve role on the asset: erc20.approve(spender, amount) with the
      spender pinned (equal_to) to THAT asset's SyrupRouter and amount `pass`.
      The pool is NOT an allowed spender.
    - One router role: deposit(assets pass, depositData equal_to deposit_data).
    - One pool role: requestRedeem(shares pass, owner=avatar) and
      redeem(shares pass, receiver=avatar, owner=avatar) — every address
      parameter pinned to the avatar, so shares and assets can never be routed
      off the Safe. NO deposit rule: entries go through the router.
    - One withdrawal-manager role:
      removeSharesById(requestId pass, sharesToRemove pass) — the manager has
      no address parameter to pin; it authenticates the owner itself and can
      only return the cancelled shares to that owner.
    Every rule is execution_options: "none" — no value, no delegatecall.
-#}
{%- from "_macros/common.tmpl" import approve -%}
roles:
{%- for token in deposit_assets %}
{%- set pool = aliases.maple["syrup_" + (token | lower) + "_pool"] -%}
{%- set router = aliases.maple["syrup_" + (token | lower) + "_router"] -%}
{%- set withdrawal_manager = aliases.maple["syrup_" + (token | lower) + "_withdrawal_manager"] %}
  # approve: the spender is the SyrupRouter, NOT the pool — the router is the
  # contract that pulls the Safe's underlying on a Syrup deposit. `amount` is
  # `pass`, which covers both the exact grant and the approve(router, 0)
  # zeroing that follows the deposit.
  {{ approve(aliases.tokens[token], router) | indent(2) }}

  - address: "{{ router }}"
    functions:
      # deposit(assets, depositData): the only route by which this policy lets
      # value leave the Safe into Syrup. `depositData` is pinned so every
      # deposit carries the agreed attribution tag and nothing else.
      - signature: "function deposit(uint256 assets, bytes32 depositData)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: assets
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: depositData
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to"
            value: "{{ deposit_data }}"
            value_type: "bytes32"

  - address: "{{ pool }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function requestRedeem(uint256 shares, address owner)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: shares
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: owner
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to_avatar"

      # redeem(shares, receiver, owner): the manual-withdrawal claim path —
      # needed when the delegate has flagged the Safe, so serviced shares sit
      # on the withdrawal manager instead of paying out.
      - signature: "function redeem(uint256 shares, address receiver, address owner)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: shares
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: receiver
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to_avatar"
          - name: owner
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to_avatar"

  # Withdrawal manager — PINNED and rotation-sensitive (see the header).
  - address: "{{ withdrawal_manager }}"
    functions:
      # removeSharesById(requestId, sharesToRemove): cancels an exact amount
      # from an exact queued request. Both params are `pass` — there is no
      # address to pin, and the manager itself requires the request's owner to
      # be the caller, so the shares can only come back to the Safe.
      - signature: "function removeSharesById(uint256 requestId, uint256 sharesToRemove)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: requestId
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: sharesToRemove
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
{%- endfor %}
